<div xmlns:perseus="http://www.perseus.org/meta/perseus.rdfs#" xmlns:dctype="http://purl.org/dc/dcmitype/" xmlns:tufts="http://www.tufts.edu/" xmlns:alph="http://alpheios.net/namespaces/tei" class="alpheios-lex-text_container en">
    <div class="alpheios-lex-entry" lemma-id="n31516" body-key=""><b><span class="alpheios-lex-la" lang="lat">nūtrītōrĭus</span></b> , a,
			um, adj.
		nutritor. <div class="alpheios-lex-lex_sense alpheios-lex-lex_sense1"><b>I.</b>
			<i>Nourishing, nutritive</i>: 
				“<span class="alpheios-lex-la" lang="lat">lac omne nutritorium est,</span>”
				 <i><span class="en">Theod. Prisc. de Diaet. 3</span></i>
			.—</div>
        <div class="alpheios-lex-lex_sense alpheios-lex-lex_sense1"><b>II.</b>
			<i>Of</i> or <i>belonging to bringing up,
				rearing</i>: 
				“<span class="alpheios-lex-la" lang="lat">cunae nutritoriae,</span>”
				 <i><span class="en">Aug. Conf. 12, 27</span></i>
			.</div>
    </div> <div class="alpheios-lex-footnotes en"> </div>
    <div class="alpheios-lex-alph-source">From <u>
            <u>A Latin Dictionary</u>
        </u> (Charlton T. Lewis, Charles Short, Oxford, Clarendon Press, 1879) </div>
